            },
            "title": "Synthesis"
          },
          {
            "text": {
              "status": "generated",
              "div": "<div xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml\">Dear Colleague, I am writing to update you regarding your patient, a 79-year-old male admitted on 03/06/2024 with symptomatic aortic valve regurgitation. On admission, he reported progressive exertional dyspnoea and mild peripheral oedema. Clinical examination confirmed signs of significant aortic regurgitation. Investigations including echocardiography, ECG, chest X-ray, and routine blood tests were performed. Echocardiography demonstrated moderate-to-severe aortic regurgitation with preserved left ventricular function. Following cardiology review and consideration of the patient's age and comorbidities, a conservative medical management approach was agreed upon. He was commenced on an ACE inhibitor and his diuretic therapy was optimised. He responded well clinically, with improvement in symptoms prior to discharge. Please ensure ongoing cardiology follow-up is arranged and monitor renal function given the medication adjustments. Do not hesitate to contact me should you require further information.</div>"
            },
